<div class="space-y-12">
    <div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-4">Persistence</h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-slate-400">A Redis 7-style append-only log and RDB snapshots &mdash; a restart brings back exactly the state the server stopped in</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Overview -->
    <div class="p-6 rounded-xl bg-indigo-500/10 border border-indigo-500/30">
        <p class="text-slate-400">The append-only log is on by default with <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">appendfsync everysec</code>. A writer thread hands each pass of records (about every millisecond) to the kernel, so a process killed with SIGKILL loses at most that last pass, and a machine crash up to about a second. Snapshots (<code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">dump.rdb</code>) are taken in the background on save points, on <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">BGSAVE</code> and at shutdown.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- How It Works -->
    <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-6">How It Works</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-6">
            <div class="p-6 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
                <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-emerald-400 mb-3">Append-only log</h3>
                <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-400">
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-400">1.</span> One log per shard; each record is appended while that shard's lock is held, so the log replays in exactly the order the shard executed.</li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-400">2.</span> Records are the <em>effect</em> on the shard: a command spanning shards writes one record per shard, relative TTLs become absolute deadlines, expirations and evictions are logged as <code class="text-slate-300">DEL</code>.</li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-400">3.</span> Transactions and scripts are bracketed <code class="text-slate-300">MULTI</code>/<code class="text-slate-300">EXEC</code>; an unfinished block at the end of the log is not applied.</li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-400">4.</span> A writer thread collects the records about every millisecond, hands them to the kernel and fsyncs per policy (always / everysec / no).</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="mt-4 p-3 rounded-lg bg-slate-900 text-xs font-mono text-slate-500">
                    Directory: &lt;dir&gt;/appendonlydir/ (manifest + base + per-shard logs)
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="p-6 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
                <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-purple-400 mb-3">Snapshots</h3>
                <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-400">
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-purple-400">1.</span> One file holding every shard and every database, with a CRC-32 checksum.</li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-purple-400">2.</span> Taken in steps: each shard is held only while its part is captured, so other clients keep being served. Lists, sets, hashes and sorted sets of 8192 elements or more are written a slice at a time (streams whole).</li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-purple-400">3.</span> Written to a temporary file, fsynced, renamed, and the directory fsynced.</li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-purple-400">4.</span> Triggered by save points (<code class="text-slate-300">--save</code>, default one change in 300 s), <code class="text-slate-300">BGSAVE</code>, <code class="text-slate-300">SAVE</code> and shutdown.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="mt-4 p-3 rounded-lg bg-slate-900 text-xs font-mono text-slate-500">
                    File: &lt;dir&gt;/dump.rdb (&lt;dbfilename&gt;.rdb)
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Rewrites -->
    <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Rewrites</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4"><code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">BGREWRITEAOF</code>, or automatically once the log has grown by <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">--auto-aof-rewrite-percentage</code> (100) and is at least <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">--auto-aof-rewrite-min-size</code> (64mb), captures each shard and switches it to a new log generation under one lock hold. Everything the new base holds is in the old generation, everything it lacks in the new one, so a restart never applies a write twice nor skips one. The manifest lists the new generation before the old files are dropped, so a crash in the middle of a rewrite still loads correctly.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Startup Sequence -->
    <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Startup Sequence</h2>
        <div class="p-6 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
            <ol class="space-y-3 text-slate-300">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                    <span class="shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-lg bg-indigo-500/20 flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold text-indigo-400">1</span>
                    <div><strong class="text-white">Choose the source</strong> &mdash; with <code class="text-slate-300">appendonly</code> on: the log named by the manifest; else a single <code class="text-slate-300">appendonly.aof</code> from an earlier version; else the snapshot. With the log off: the snapshot. The snapshot is never replayed under the log (that applied every logged write twice in 0.4).</div>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                    <span class="shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-lg bg-indigo-500/20 flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold text-indigo-400">2</span>
                    <div><strong class="text-white">Replay</strong> &mdash; records are streamed, never collected in memory. A record cut off by a crash at the very end is dropped and truncated away; anything else that does not parse stops startup.</div>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                    <span class="shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-lg bg-indigo-500/20 flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold text-indigo-400">3</span>
                    <div><strong class="text-white">Re-hash</strong> &mdash; every key moves to the shard that owns it under the current configuration, so <code class="text-slate-300">--shards</code> can change between runs. If the layout changed, the log is rewritten before the first client connects.</div>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                    <span class="shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-lg bg-indigo-500/20 flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold text-indigo-400">4</span>
                    <div><strong class="text-white">Start serving</strong> &mdash; RESP and REST accept connections; new writes go to the log.</div>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Fsync Policies -->
    <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Fsync Policies</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead><tr class="border-b border-white/10"><th class="py-3 pr-4 text-slate-400 font-medium">Policy</th><th class="py-3 pr-4 text-slate-400 font-medium">Process killed (SIGKILL, OOM)</th><th class="py-3 pr-4 text-slate-400 font-medium">Machine crash / power loss</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody class="text-slate-300">
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">always</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">Nothing acknowledged</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">Replies are held until the fsync that covers them, as in Redis; replies produced meanwhile share one fsync (group commit)</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">everysec</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">At most the last writer pass (~1 ms)</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">Up to about one second. Default</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">no</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">At most the last writer pass (~1 ms)</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">Whatever the OS had not flushed</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Configuration -->
    <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Configuration</h2>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-white/10 overflow-hidden">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 border-b border-white/5">
                <span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-mono">Terminal</span>
            </div>
            <div class="p-4 font-mono text-sm space-y-1">
                <div class="text-slate-500"># Data directory and fsync policy</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">solikv --dir /var/lib/solikv --appendfsync always</div>
                <div class="text-slate-500 mt-3"># Redis save points: after 1 change in 3600 s, or 100 changes in 300 s</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">solikv --save "3600 1 300 100"</div>
                <div class="text-slate-500 mt-3"># Snapshots only (no log); "" disables periodic snapshots too</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">solikv --appendonly false</div>
                <div class="text-slate-500 mt-3"># At runtime</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">redis-cli CONFIG SET appendonly yes</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">redis-cli CONFIG SET appendfsync everysec</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">redis-cli CONFIG SET save "900 1"</div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-500">Turning the log on at runtime starts it from the current dataset with a rewrite; turning it off keeps the existing files. Both are refused while a rewrite runs.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Persistence Commands -->
    <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Persistence Commands</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead><tr class="border-b border-white/10"><th class="py-3 pr-4 text-slate-400 font-medium">Command</th><th class="py-3 text-slate-400 font-medium">Description</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody class="text-slate-300">
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">SAVE</td><td class="py-3">Snapshot now and reply when done. Other clients keep being served, except in <code class="text-slate-300">--solo</code> mode where, as in Redis, the server waits</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">BGSAVE</td><td class="py-3">Snapshot in the background (<code class="text-slate-300">Background saving started</code>)</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">BGREWRITEAOF</td><td class="py-3">Compact the log in the background</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">LASTSAVE</td><td class="py-3">Unix time of the last successful snapshot</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">SHUTDOWN [NOSAVE|SAVE]</td><td class="py-3">Stop writes, fsync the log, snapshot (when save points are set, or with <code class="text-slate-300">SAVE</code>), exit. SIGTERM and SIGINT do the same.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-indigo-400">INFO persistence</td><td class="py-3">Snapshot and rewrite status, log sizes</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Migrating from Redis -->
    <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Migrating from Redis</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 mb-4">SoliKV imports Redis <code class="text-indigo-400 bg-indigo-500/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-xs">dump.rdb</code> files at startup, Redis 7.4's (RDB version 12) included. Each key keeps its Redis database; keys already expired are left out.</p>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-white/10 overflow-hidden mb-4">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 border-b border-white/5">
                <span class="text-xs text-slate-500 font-mono">Terminal</span>
            </div>
            <div class="p-4 font-mono text-sm space-y-1">
                <div class="text-slate-500"># 1. Generate a dump on your Redis instance</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">redis-cli BGSAVE</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">cp /var/lib/redis/dump.rdb /tmp/dump.rdb</div>
                <div class="text-slate-500 mt-3"># 2. Import into SoliKV</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">solikv --import-redis-rdb /tmp/dump.rdb --bind 127.0.0.1</div>
                <div class="text-slate-500 mt-3"># 3. Verify your data</div>
                <div class="text-emerald-400">redis-cli DBSIZE</div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4">
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-emerald-400 mb-2">Imported</h4>
                <ul class="space-y-1 text-sm text-slate-400">
                    <li>&#10003; Strings</li>
                    <li>&#10003; Lists (plain, ziplist, quicklist, quicklist v2)</li>
                    <li>&#10003; Sets (plain, intset, listpack)</li>
                    <li>&#10003; Sorted sets (plain, ziplist, listpack, binary scores)</li>
                    <li>&#10003; Hashes (plain, ziplist, listpack)</li>
                    <li>&#10003; Hashes with field expiry (Redis 7.4)</li>
                    <li>&#10003; LZF-compressed values, key expiry, databases</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-amber-400 mb-2">Skipped (with a warning)</h4>
                <ul class="space-y-1 text-sm text-slate-400">
                    <li>&mdash; Streams</li>
                    <li>&mdash; Modules</li>
                    <li>&mdash; zipmap hashes (Redis &lt; 2.6)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-500"><code class="text-slate-300">DUMP</code>/<code class="text-slate-300">RESTORE</code> payloads also use Redis' format for strings, lists, sets, hashes and sorted sets, so single keys move in both directions.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Upgrading -->
    <div class="p-6 rounded-xl bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/30">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Upgrading from 0.4.x</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-400">
            <li>&bull; A single <code class="text-slate-300">appendonly.aof</code> is loaded, rewritten into <code class="text-slate-300">appendonlydir/</code> and kept as <code class="text-slate-300">appendonly.aof.migrated</code>.</li>
            <li>&bull; Per-shard <code class="text-slate-300">dump-&lt;n&gt;.rdb</code> files are still read, for any shard count; the next snapshot is the single <code class="text-slate-300">dump.rdb</code>. <code class="text-slate-300">--dbfilename</code> is now the base name of <code class="text-slate-300">&lt;name&gt;.rdb</code>.</li>
            <li>&bull; Data written by 0.4 loads into database 0.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- File Layout -->
    <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">File Layout</h2>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-white/10 p-6">
            <pre class="text-sm font-mono text-slate-300 overflow-x-auto">
data/
  dump.rdb                          # snapshot: all shards and databases, CRC-32
  appendonlydir/
    appendonly.manifest             # which files make up the log, in order
    appendonly.&lt;gen&gt;.base.rdb       # snapshot the log starts from
    appendonly.&lt;gen&gt;.incr.&lt;s&gt;.aof   # RESP records for shard &lt;s&gt;
  solikv.log                        # daemon mode log (--logfile)</pre>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-500">The data directory is created with mode 0700 and the files with 0600.</p>
    </div>
</div>
